UNDO for PostgreSQL
===================

This directory implements a cluster-wide UNDO engine: a way for a
transaction to record how to reverse a change it has made, so that the
change is reversed if the transaction rolls back, or if a crash leaves it
in progress.  The first consumer is FILEOPS, the transactional filesystem
operations in storage/file/fileops.c; see storage/file/README.

Records and chains
------------------

A consumer writes UNDO through xactundo.c.  PrepareXactUndoData() appends a
record (an UndoRecordHeader, undorecord.h, and an opaque payload) to the
transaction's UndoRecordSet for the record's persistence level, and
InsertXactUndoData() writes the set as one XLOG_UNDO_BATCH WAL record,
inside the critical section of the consumer's own WAL record.  There are no
UNDO files: the records live in the WAL stream.

Each batch carries the LSN of the transaction's previous batch
(xl_undo_batch.chain_prev), so a transaction's UNDO forms a chain from its
newest batch back to its first.  Every record carries the top-level xid,
even one written by a subtransaction.  urec_rmid names the UNDO resource
manager that applies the record (undormgr.h); consumers register theirs
through access/undormgrlist.h, and the engine never interprets a payload.

A record is applied by handing it to its resource manager's rm_undo
callback.  The engine keeps no "already applied" marker, and the same
record can be applied more than once, so a callback must leave alone an
object that no longer shows the record's effect.  A callback must not
write WAL, since redo of XLOG_UNDO_ROLLBACK (below) calls it.

Rollback
--------

AbortTransaction() calls AtAbort_XactUndo(), which walks the chain from its
newest batch, applying each batch's records newest first
(ApplyUndoChainFromWAL(), undoapply.c).  A subtransaction abort applies only
the batches written since the subtransaction started, and a subtransaction
commit hands its chain to the parent.  ROLLBACK PREPARED applies the chain
whose head PREPARE saved in the 2PC state.

A consumer decides how its rollback reaches a standby.  FILEOPS carries its
inverses in the abort record and applies them from there, so the
transaction-abort walk skips its records (FileOpsSkipUndo).

Crash recovery
--------------

During redo, each XLOG_UNDO_BATCH record registers its transaction in an
in-memory table (undo_xlog.c), and the transaction's commit or abort
record removes it.  Redo starts at the checkpoint's redo pointer, so it
replays none of the batches written before it; each online checkpoint
therefore writes an XLOG_UNDO_INFLIGHT record listing the chain heads of
the transactions in progress (LogXactUndoInflight()), which redo adds to
the table.  InsertXactUndoData() holds off the checkpoint's redo-pointer
phase while it inserts a batch and publishes its chain head, so no batch
can fall between the two.

What remains in the table at the end of redo, apart from prepared
transactions, are the transactions the crash left in progress.
StartupXLOG() calls UndoRecoveryRollBack() once WAL is writable, just
before the end-of-recovery checkpoint or record.  For each such loser it
applies the UNDO records, newest first, then writes an XLOG_UNDO_ROLLBACK
record carrying the records that applied, and forgets the loser.  Redo of
that record applies the same records again and forgets the loser too.  So
a standby rolls the loser back at the same point in the WAL as the primary
did, without reading the loser's chain (which a restartpoint may already
have removed), and a later recovery never reverts it a second time.

A crash before the rollback record reaches disk leaves the loser tracked,
and the next recovery rolls it back again.  A record whose callback fails
is left out of the rollback record, so the change it describes stays in
place everywhere, and the summary at the end of the undo phase says how
many records could not be applied.

Recovery applies only PERMANENT records: temporary relations do not
survive a restart, and unlogged ones are reset to their init forks.

WAL retention
-------------

Each backend publishes the LSN of its current transaction's first UNDO
batch in a shared slot (undolog.c), and a prepared transaction's chain
head is kept in its 2PC state.  KeepLogSeg() keeps WAL from the oldest of
them onward, so a long-running or long-prepared transaction that wrote
UNDO holds back WAL removal until it ends, much as a replication slot
does.

Files
-----

    undo.c          shared memory set-up and per-backend initialization
    undolog.c       per-backend first-batch LSN slots for WAL retention
    undorecord.c    UndoRecordHeader and UndoRecordSet
    undoinsert.c    writing an UndoRecordSet as an XLOG_UNDO_BATCH record
    xactundo.c      per-transaction UNDO, the checkpoint in-flight record
    undoapply.c     online rollback: walking a chain and applying it
    undo_xlog.c     RM_UNDO_ID redo, the recovery undo phase, batch reads
    undormgr.c      UNDO resource manager registry
